1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,626
January 1521. A sharp frost.
All night, priests chant over

2
00:00:05,626 --> 00:00:09,703
a pool dug into the earth
and lined with planks.

3
00:00:10,153 --> 00:00:13,828
Before dawn, the emperor of
Ethiopia goes down into the

4
00:00:13,828 --> 00:00:16,702
icy water. Then his
bishop. Then the queen.

5
00:00:17,152 --> 00:00:19,925
A Portuguese priest, Francisco Alvares, is

6
00:00:19,925 --> 00:00:22,037
watching. He writes it all down.

7
00:00:22,487 --> 00:00:25,687
Why were they in the water
at all? Follow for Part 2.

8
00:00:27,637 --> 00:00:31,990
Every year on Tir eleven, the
nineteenth of January, Ethiopia

9
00:00:31,990 --> 00:00:35,915
goes down to the water. The
feast is Timkat: immersion.

10
00:00:36,365 --> 00:00:39,640
The eve is called Ketera.
It means making the pool:

11
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:42,466
villages dam a stream
so the river is ready.

12
00:00:42,916 --> 00:00:46,073
In the afternoon the bells
ring, and the tabot comes out.

13
00:00:46,523 --> 00:00:49,967
No one sees it. Wrapped in
embroidered cloth, it rides

14
00:00:49,967 --> 00:00:52,710
on a priest's head,
under bright umbrellas.

15
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:56,467
It is the only thing in the
church anointed with holy oil.

16
00:00:56,917 --> 00:00:59,827
Drums, incense, singing.
So why do people dance

17
00:00:59,827 --> 00:01:01,994
in front of it? Comment your guess.

18
00:01:03,944 --> 00:01:06,781
Did you know? The dancing
has a scripture behind it.

19
00:01:07,231 --> 00:01:10,699
When the Ark came up to
Jerusalem, David danced before

20
00:01:10,699 --> 00:01:13,717
the LORD with all his
might. Second Samuel six.

21
00:01:14,167 --> 00:01:17,175
And the walk to the water
recalls another road:

22
00:01:17,175 --> 00:01:19,671
Jesus going from Galilee to the Jordan.

23
00:01:20,121 --> 00:01:23,363
So what happens at the water,
all night long? Save this one.

24
00:01:25,313 --> 00:01:27,752
Each tabot rests in its
own tent beside the

25
00:01:27,752 --> 00:01:29,964
water. Priests chant through the night.

26
00:01:30,414 --> 00:01:34,634
Alvares saw the same in 1521.
The priests never ceased

27
00:01:34,634 --> 00:01:38,777
chanting over the tank, he
wrote, blessing the water.

28
00:01:39,227 --> 00:01:41,548
His pool was squared in the earth, lined

29
00:01:41,548 --> 00:01:43,579
with planks and waxed cotton cloth.

30
00:01:44,029 --> 00:01:47,405
Its water ran down a channel
and was strained through a

31
00:01:47,405 --> 00:01:50,535
bag. Oranges and lemons
hung over it like a garden.

32
00:01:50,985 --> 00:01:54,134
Before sunrise comes the
liturgy. The Gospels are

33
00:01:54,134 --> 00:01:56,447
read at the four sides of the water.

34
00:01:56,897 --> 00:01:59,401
The cross is dipped. Then comes the moment

35
00:01:59,401 --> 00:02:01,249
the whole crowd has waited for.

36
00:02:03,199 --> 00:02:05,782
The priests sprinkle the crowd. Hands go

37
00:02:05,782 --> 00:02:07,977
up. Children shriek. Some jump in.

38
00:02:08,427 --> 00:02:11,445
Alvares thought he was
watching a second baptism. He

39
00:02:11,445 --> 00:02:14,230
told the emperor his Church
had nothing like it.

40
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:19,948
In 1814, Henry Salt asked a man
who had watched it every year. The

41
00:02:19,948 --> 00:02:24,259
answer: a mere commemoration
of Our Saviour's baptism.

42
00:02:24,709 --> 00:02:28,445
One baptism, as the Creed
says. Timkat remembers His. So

43
00:02:28,445 --> 00:02:31,514
what did the emperor ask
Alvares that morning?

44
00:02:31,964 --> 00:02:34,993
He asked: are there any
Portuguese here who can swim?

45
00:02:35,443 --> 00:02:38,464
Two jumped in, and swam
and dived. The emperor,

46
00:02:38,464 --> 00:02:40,649
Alvares wrote, enjoyed it greatly.

47
00:02:42,599 --> 00:02:46,031
Did you know? In Gondar, a
stone pool sits empty all

48
00:02:46,031 --> 00:02:48,935
year, roots of old trees
gripping its walls.

49
00:02:49,385 --> 00:02:52,265
It is filled once, for
Timkat. People call it

50
00:02:52,265 --> 00:02:55,337
Fasilides' Bath. Nobody
is certain who built it.

51
00:02:55,787 --> 00:03:01,141
In 2019, UNESCO listed Timkat
as heritage of all humanity.

52
00:03:01,591 --> 00:03:05,751
And when the feast is over, one
tabot does not go home. Which one?

53
00:03:07,701 --> 00:03:11,146
Saint Michael's. His feast
falls the next day, and the

54
00:03:11,146 --> 00:03:14,144
Church remembers Cana,
where water became wine.

55
00:03:14,594 --> 00:03:18,923
The other tabots go home
with songs. Young men play

56
00:03:18,923 --> 00:03:22,487
genna, as a traveller saw at Adwa in 1853.

57
00:03:22,937 --> 00:03:26,564
And a young man may toss a lemon
to a girl, to say he likes her.

58
00:03:28,514 --> 00:03:33,564
So look at what we found. A pool
of planks in 1521, a visitor

59
00:03:33,564 --> 00:03:37,538
who misread it, and a city
pool no one can date.

60
00:03:37,988 --> 00:03:40,796
When someone tells you what
a feast means, ask the

61
00:03:40,796 --> 00:03:43,492
people who keep it, and
who first wrote it down.

62
00:03:45,442 --> 00:03:48,555
And Jesus, when he was
baptized, went up straightway

63
00:03:48,555 --> 00:03:51,010
out of the water. Matthew three, sixteen.

64
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:53,822
Some come only for the spectacle and the

65
00:03:53,822 --> 00:03:56,303
photos. Some treat the water like a charm.

66
00:03:56,753 --> 00:04:00,123
The quiet way: keep the fast
of the eve, stand in the

67
00:04:00,123 --> 00:04:03,494
vigil, and remember the
promises of your own baptism.

68
00:04:03,944 --> 00:04:07,592
Then carry a little blessed water
home to an elder, and pray together.

69
00:04:09,542 --> 00:04:12,998
Melkam Timkat. Thank you for
sitting with us over coffee.

70
00:04:13,448 --> 00:04:14,920
This is Ethiopics.
